          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-240v-generator/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is 220V the same as 240V on a generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, for practical purposes. North American residential split-phase power is nominally 120/240V, and 220V and 230V are older or informal names for the same service. A generator listed as 240V, 220V or 120/240V with an L14-30R or 14-50R outlet will run the same appliances."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the smallest generator with 240V?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Open-frame generators with an L14-30R start at roughly 4,500-5,000 running watts, such as the DuroMax XP5500HX and Westinghouse WGen5300DFcv. Among inverters, true 240V usually starts around 5,000-5,500 running watts, with the Champion 100519, Yamaha EF6300iSDE and Honda EU7000iS. Most 4,500-watt-class inverters are 120V only."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I run a 240V well pump on a portable generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, if the generator has a 120/240V outlet and enough surge capacity for the pump. A 5,000-6,000 running watt generator with an L14-30R handles a common residential pump with some other loads. Connect it through a transfer switch, not a back-fed outlet, and check the pump's label for its starting current."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a transfer switch for a 240V generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If you are feeding your house wiring, yes, or a listed interlock kit with an inlet box. It keeps the generator and utility from being connected at the same time, which protects line workers and your generator. Plugging appliances directly into the generator with cords does not need one."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use an adapter to get 240V from an L5-30 outlet?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. An L5-30 provides a single 120V hot, a neutral and a ground. No adapter can create the second hot leg that 240V requires. Adapters that claim to do this are either mislabeled or dangerous. You need a generator with an L14-30R or 14-50R outlet."
          }
        },
