    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          You need a 240V generator if you are on a well, want to run central air or an electric dryer, or plan to feed your panel through a transfer switch. You need an L14-30R or 14-50R outlet to get it. An L5-30 or TT-30 is 120V only, no matter how much it looks like a 240V plug.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="which-loads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Which Loads Actually Need 240 Volts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most of what you plug in at home runs on 120V: refrigerators, lights, TVs, microwaves, window air conditioners, furnace blowers and sump pumps. A generator with only 120V outlets can run all of them. The reason to look for a 240V generator is a short list of heavy appliances wired to double-pole breakers in your panel, and the first job is working out whether any of them matter to you in an outage.
          </p>

          <p>
            The well pump is the most common reason. Many submersible well pumps run on 240V, and without the pump there is no water to drink, cook with or flush toilets. If you are on a well, a 240V generator is not optional. Our <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-a-well-pump/">well pump generator sizing guide</a> covers the starting surge, which is the number that decides generator size.
          </p>

          <p>
            Central air conditioning and heat pumps are next. The outdoor condenser almost always runs on 240V and has a large compressor starting surge. It is possible to run one on a large portable, and <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-central-air-conditioning/">what size generator runs central air</a> gives the numbers, but it pushes you into the 9,000-watt-plus range or requires a soft starter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric dryers, ranges, ovens and water heaters are 240V resistive loads that draw a lot of current. They work on a generator, but each one can use most of a mid-size unit's 240V capacity on its own. A water heater element alone can take a large share of a 5,000-watt generator, as our <a href="/can-a-portable-generator-power-an-electric-water-heater/">electric water heater guide</a> explains. In practice, most people run these one at a time, briefly, or not at all during an outage.
          </p>

          <p>
            EV charging is the newest 240V load people ask about. It can work at a low charging rate, with caveats we cover below. And if you want to back-feed your whole panel through a transfer switch or interlock, you need 240V even if every appliance you care about is 120V, because the panel has two 120V legs and a 120V-only generator can only feed one of them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outlets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. L14-30 vs 14-50 vs L5-30: Reading the Outlet Panel
          </h2>

          <p>
            Generator outlet panels decide what you can connect, and the labels look similar enough to cause expensive mistakes. The NEMA code tells you everything. The 'L' means locking twist-lock, the first number is the configuration, and the second number is the amperage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">L14-30R: the standard 240V generator outlet</h3>

          <p>
            The L14-30R is a 4-prong, 30-amp twist-lock with two hots, a neutral and a ground. It supplies 120/240V split-phase power, up to 7,200 watts in theory and less in practice because of generator ratings. It is the outlet most manual transfer switches and power inlet boxes are designed around, and it is what you want on a generator meant to back-feed a house. Every generator on this list has one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">14-50R: the 50-amp outlet</h3>

          <p>
            The 14-50R is a 4-prong, 50-amp, 120/240V outlet. It is not twist-lock. It can carry up to 12,000 watts and shows up on generators of roughly 9,000 running watts and above. It connects to 50-amp inlets and 50-amp RVs. If that outlet is specifically what you are shopping for, our <a href="/best-generator-with-50-amp-outlet/">50-amp outlet generator guide</a> goes deeper. For most houses, an L14-30 is enough.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">L5-30R and TT-30R: 120V only</h3>

          <p>
            This is where people get caught. The L5-30R is a 3-prong, 30-amp twist-lock that looks like a 240V outlet but is 120V only: one hot, one neutral, one ground. The TT-30R is the 120V RV outlet. Neither can run a 240V appliance or feed both legs of your panel, no matter what adapter you buy. Generators that list only L5-30 and TT-30 outlets are 120V generators.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Outlet</th>
