        <div id="split-phase" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Split-Phase, Selector Switches and Full-Power Modes
          </h2>

          <p>
            North American homes get split-phase power: two 120V legs, opposite in phase, so the voltage between them is 240V. A 240V generator reproduces that with two windings, or with an inverter that synthesizes both legs. Each leg can carry about half the generator's rated output.
          </p>

          <p>
            That half matters. On a 5,300-watt generator, each 120V leg can carry roughly 2,650 watts. If you plug everything into outlets on one leg, you can trip that leg's breaker while the generator still has capacity on the other. When you connect through a transfer switch, balance the circuits so heavy 120V loads are split between the two legs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many 240V generators include a 120/240V voltage selector switch. In the 120/240V position, the 240V outlet works and the 120V outlets share the two legs. In the 120V-only position, the generator puts both windings in parallel so its full output is available on the 120V outlets, which is useful for a big 120V load like an RV air conditioner, but the 240V outlet goes dead. Westinghouse markets several of its WGen models with this selector, and Honda says the EU7000iS can deliver full power through its 120V outlets this way. Leave it in 120/240V mode if a transfer switch or well pump is connected.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inverter generators handle split phase differently. Many small inverters produce a single 120V output and cannot make 240V at all. Larger inverters with a 240V outlet either synthesize two legs internally or combine two inverter modules. Parallel kits that link two small 120V inverters add 120V capacity but do not create 240V. Two 2,200-watt inverters in parallel still have no 240V outlet.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">240V needs four wires: two hots, a neutral and a ground.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the outlet has three prongs, it is not a split-phase 240V outlet, whatever the listing says. Look for L14-30R or 14-50R on the spec sheet.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="smallest" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Smallest Generators With True 240V
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you need 240V only for a well pump, you do not need a 10,000-watt generator. You need the smallest generator that has an L14-30R and enough surge for the pump. That turns out to be a short list, because manufacturers generally do not put 240V outlets on generators below roughly 4,500-5,000 running watts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Among open-frames, budget dual-fuel units around 4,500-5,300 running watts are where 240V starts. The DuroMax XP5500HX, which we cover in our <a href="/best-cheap-generator/">cheap generator guide</a>, lists a 120/240V 30A twist-lock at 4,500 running watts. The Westinghouse WGen5300DFcv on this list adds a CO sensor and 5,300 running watts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Among inverters, the 4,000-4,500 watt models most people buy are 120V only. The Westinghouse iGen4500 family, for example, has a 5-20R duplex and a TT-30R but no 240V outlet. True 240V inverters start around 5,000-5,500 running watts: the Champion 100519 at 5,000 running watts, Yamaha's EF6300iSDE at 5,500 rated watts, and the Honda EU7000iS at 5,500 rated watts all list an L14-30R. Above that, models such as Champion's 100520 add more output in the same open-frame inverter format.
          </p>

          <p>
            Small 240V generators have one real limit: current per leg. A 5,000-watt generator provides roughly 20-22 amps at 240V. That is enough for a typical well pump with some 120V load alongside, but not for a dryer and a pump at the same time. Size from your largest 240V load's starting surge, not from the outlet rating. The <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> lists typical figures.
          </p>

