        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Sizing for a Well Pump, Dryer, Range or Central AC
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sizing a 240V generator works the same way as any generator, with one extra step. Add the running watts of everything you will run at once, then add the starting surge of the largest motor. Then check that each 240V appliance's current fits within the generator's rated 240V amps.
          </p>

          <p>
            A well pump is a motor load with a large starting surge. The pump's horsepower and type decide how much, and pump motors can draw several times their running current for a moment at start. That surge is why some generators that look big enough on running watts still stall when the pump kicks on. A generator in the 5,000-6,000 running watt class with an L14-30R typically handles a common residential pump plus a refrigerator and lights, but check your pump's label and our well pump guide before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric dryers and ranges are resistive loads, so there is no surge, but the draw is large. A dryer's heating element alone can use a large share of a 5,000-watt generator, and a range with several burners on can exceed it. On a small 240V generator, run these one at a time and briefly, or skip them during outages. On a 9,500-watt-plus generator, one of them at a time is reasonable alongside essential loads.
          </p>

          <p>
            Central AC is the hardest 240V load to start. Compressor surge often exceeds what a mid-size generator can deliver, and the condenser draws a lot while running too. Plan for a large generator like the WGen9500DFc or DuroMax XP13000EH, and consider a soft-start kit on the condenser, which many owners install specifically so a portable can start it. For whole-home comfort on a portable, see the <a href="/best-10000-watt-generator/">10,000 watt generator</a> and <a href="/best-12000-watt-generator/">12,000 watt generator</a> guides.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your list of 240V loads includes everything, a portable may be the wrong tool. A standby generator wired to a whole-house transfer switch handles central air, range, dryer and well pump without cords. <a href="/best-whole-house-generator/">Whole house generators</a> and <a href="/portable-vs-standby-generator/">portable vs standby</a> cover that decision.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ev" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. EV Charging From a 240V Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            Charging an electric car from a portable generator is possible and, in an extended outage, sometimes useful. It is not efficient, and it does not always work the first time.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical approach is Level 2 charging at a reduced rate. Most Level 2 chargers and many cars let you limit charging current. At 16 amps and 240V, the car draws about 3,840 watts, which a 5,000-watt-plus 240V generator can sustain with headroom for a refrigerator. At the full 32 or 48 amps many chargers default to, the charger will overload a portable generator. Set the limit before you plug in.
          </p>

          <p>
            The common problem is grounding. Many EV chargers check for a proper ground-to-neutral relationship and refuse to charge if they do not see one. Portable generators may have a bonded or floating neutral depending on the model, and owners commonly report chargers faulting on generator power for this reason. The fix depends on how the generator connects. A generator feeding the house through a transfer switch often behaves differently from one plugged directly into a portable charger. Check the charger maker's guidance before relying on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep expectations realistic. A few hours on a generator adds modest range at a fuel cost per mile far higher than grid charging. It is an emergency measure to get to work or a charger, not a substitute for the grid. And never charge with the generator anywhere near an attached garage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="transfer-switch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. The Transfer Switch Is Not Optional
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 240V generator's main job is usually feeding the house panel, and there is exactly one safe way to do that: through a transfer switch or a listed interlock kit, with a power inlet box outside. Plugging a generator into a dryer outlet with a double-male cord, the so-called suicide cord, back-feeds the utility lines. That can electrocute line workers, energize your neighbors' wiring, and destroy the generator when power comes back.
          </p>

          <p>
            A manual transfer switch installs next to your main panel and moves selected circuits from utility to generator power. It typically comes with an L14-30 or 14-50 inlet and cord. Our <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">best manual transfer switch</a> guide covers the options. An interlock kit is a less expensive alternative that mechanically prevents the main breaker and a generator breaker from being on at the same time, so you can feed the whole panel and choose circuits at the breakers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Either way, the connection needs to be rated for your generator's outlet. A 30-amp inlet pairs with an L14-30 generator. A 50-amp inlet pairs with a 14-50 or a 50-amp twist-lock. Many jurisdictions require a permit and a licensed electrician for this work, and some utilities require notification. Budget for it; <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch types and installation cost</a> gives typical ranges.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neutral bonding is the detail electricians care about most. Your house panel bonds neutral to ground at the service. If your transfer switch does not switch the neutral, the generator generally should have a floating neutral to avoid a second bond. If it does switch the neutral, a bonded-neutral generator is typically the right match. Follow the generator and switch manuals, and ask the electrician. Our <a href="/do-i-need-to-ground-a-portable-generator/">grounding guide</a> explains the basics.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cords-placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cords, Inlets and Placement
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 240V generator connection uses one heavy cord instead of a tangle of extension cords, which is a large part of its appeal. That cord has to match the outlet and be heavy enough for the run. An L14-30 generator cord is typically 10-gauge, 4-conductor, with an L14-30P on one end and an L14-30R connector on the other. A 50-amp cord is heavier, usually 6-gauge. Our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">generator cord size guide</a> gives gauges by length and load.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the cord as short as placement allows, but never at the expense of distance from the house. Run the generator at least 20 feet from doors, windows and vents, with the exhaust pointed away. Our <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">generator placement guide</a> covers wind and rain. A longer cord with the right gauge is always better than a generator next to a window.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mount the inlet box where you can roll the generator to that spot and still meet the distance rules. Many people install the inlet on the side of the house closest to the panel and then discover that location puts the generator under a bedroom window. Pick the generator location first, then the inlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, test the whole system before you need it. Run the generator through the transfer switch, start the well pump, check the voltage on both legs, and confirm every circuit you care about works. A first test during an ice storm is a bad time to find out the cord is the wrong plug type. If you are still choosing a size, the <a href="/best-8000-watt-generator/">8,000 watt generator</a> and <a href="/best-5000-watt-generator/">5,000 watt generator</a> guides cover the most common 240V classes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mistaking an L5-30 for a 240V outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The L5-30R is a 3-prong twist-lock that looks a lot like an L14-30R, but it is 120V only. Plenty of buyers order a generator with an L5-30 expecting to run a well pump or feed a transfer switch, then find out it cannot. If the outlet does not have four prongs, it is not split-phase 240V.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Back-feeding through a dryer outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A double-male cord into a dryer receptacle back-feeds the utility lines, can electrocute line workers and can destroy the generator when grid power returns. It is illegal in most places for good reason. Use a transfer switch or a listed interlock kit with an inlet box, installed to code.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing for running watts and ignoring pump surge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A well pump or AC compressor can draw several times its running current at start. A generator that covers the running watts but not the surge will stall or trip every time the pump kicks on. Size from the largest starting surge plus everything else running at the time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the selector in 120V-only mode</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many 240V generators have a voltage selector. In 120V-only mode the 240V outlet is dead, so the well pump and anything on a double-pole breaker will not run. Set it to 120/240V when feeding a transfer switch, and label the switch so nobody changes it in the dark.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring leg balance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each 120V leg of a split-phase generator carries roughly half the rated output. Put all your heavy 120V loads on one leg and you will trip that side while the generator has spare capacity on the other. When you choose transfer switch circuits, split the refrigerator, freezer and sump pump between legs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

