        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 220V the same as 240V on a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for practical purposes. North American residential split-phase power is nominally 120/240V, and 220V and 230V are older or informal names for the same service. A generator listed as 240V, 220V or 120/240V with an L14-30R or 14-50R outlet will run the same appliances.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the smallest generator with 240V?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Open-frame generators with an L14-30R start at roughly 4,500-5,000 running watts, such as the DuroMax XP5500HX and Westinghouse WGen5300DFcv. Among inverters, true 240V usually starts around 5,000-5,500 running watts, with the Champion 100519, Yamaha EF6300iSDE and Honda EU7000iS. Most 4,500-watt-class inverters are 120V only.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a 240V well pump on a portable generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the generator has a 120/240V outlet and enough surge capacity for the pump. A 5,000-6,000 running watt generator with an L14-30R handles a common residential pump with some other loads. Connect it through a transfer switch, not a back-fed outlet, and check the pump's label for its starting current.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a transfer switch for a 240V generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you are feeding your house wiring, yes, or a listed interlock kit with an inlet box. It keeps the generator and utility from being connected at the same time, which protects line workers and your generator. Plugging appliances directly into the generator with cords does not need one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use an adapter to get 240V from an L5-30 outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. An L5-30 provides a single 120V hot, a neutral and a ground. No adapter can create the second hot leg that 240V requires. Adapters that claim to do this are either mislabeled or dangerous. You need a generator with an L14-30R or 14-50R outlet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 240V generator charge an electric car?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, at a reduced rate. Set your Level 2 charger to around 16 amps so the load stays near 3,800 watts, and expect some chargers to refuse generator power because of how the neutral and ground are bonded. It is slow and expensive per mile, so treat it as an emergency option.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is L14-30 or 14-50 better for home backup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                L14-30 is enough for most homes running a well pump and essential circuits, and it is the most common transfer switch connection. A 14-50 carries more current and suits generators above about 9,000 running watts powering central air or several big 240V loads. Match the outlet to your inlet and transfer switch rating.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
